    "docs": {
        "PRODUCT_SPEC.md": {
            "max_bytes": 705982,
            "reason": "raised 2026-08-18 from 703126 to 705982, one byte above the file's measured 705981, for 2856 bytes. By requirement: 1703 for the new Requirement 311; 101 for Requirement 230, which took a case and a closing criterion and gave back 99 by dropping its restatement of four language laws for a pointer at their home; 209 for Requirement 232, a case, a criterion, and a second restatement swapped for a pointer; 186 each for Requirements 293 and 294; 221 for Requirement 298, whose subject is the installer this change rewrote; 79 for Requirement 246, whose prose asked that every chat judge be wired; 27 for Requirement 295, whose context gave the code-anchor Stop scan as what survives its own retirement; and 144 for the generated code-to-location table, which follows the new criteria's anchors. What earns the growth: the six background chat checks the owner unwired on 2026-08-17 are opt-in from that day, no requirement said so, and six requirements went on promising a verdict or an install the default walk no longer delivers. The closed portion could not rotate instead, since every one of them is live law about hooks that ship today. Before that: ratcheted down 2026-08-17 to 703126 \u2014 one byte above the file's measured 703125. It stood at 840000, raised 2026-07-20 for the back-describe migration (v3.0.0, INV-239/INV-217) when the Formal index gained its permanent Description column; that migration has long since landed and left 136875 bytes of unspent room, so the room is withdrawn. One byte above rather than exactly at the size, because tests/test_doc_bound.py asserts each of the four large docs sits strictly under its ceiling. Growth from here is paid for by name: raise this number with a reason that says what was added and why the closed portion could not rotate instead."
        },
--
            "reason": "reconciled 2026-08-18 at the merge of two independent same-day raises into main, one byte above the merged file's measured 483286. The judges package raised 484267 by 354 bytes across six rows (M-465, M-460, M-461, M-392, M-384, M-420 \u2014 the day the six background chat checks went opt-in and their owning rows had to say so); the spec-split package separately raised the same 484267 base by 130 bytes for the M-204 reword (the retired embedded-`## Reference`-duplicate test dropped from its owning-test list). Both increases are real and both landed \u2014 TEST_MATRIX.md carries both edits \u2014 but the same day a third package (matrix/2026-08-18-narratives) rotated thirteen rows' incident narratives out of this file's fact cells into docs/matrix-notes/, a reduction neither raise's arithmetic saw. The ceiling here is not the two raises added to the old base; it is one byte above the real merged file's measured size, which nets all three changes. Before the two raises: ratcheted down 2026-08-17 to 484268 \u2014 one byte above the file's measured 484267. It stood at 530000, raised 2026-07-28 on a measurement over the live document: the matrix carries no row whose anchor has left PRODUCT_SPEC.md or ARCHITECTURE.md, so a rotation would move zero rows and free no bytes, and the document grew because the covered surface grew. That reading still holds for the rows still living here \u2014 the narratives move was a separate remedy for the rows it named. One byte above rather than exactly at the size, because tests/test_doc_bound.py asserts the strict inequality."
        },
        "JOURNAL.md": {
            "max_bytes": 376562,
            "reason": "raised 2026-08-18 from 373267 to 376562, one byte above the file's measured 376561, for the 3295 bytes of one chapter: the day six background judges went opt-in, why the declaration moved, what the two rules that lost their last machine have left, and the one installer run the owner owes the machine before this pushes. A journal chapter records the day it happened and cannot be held anywhere else; rotation was not the remedy because the August chapters are the live ones. Before that: ratcheted down 2026-08-17 to 373267 \u2014 one byte above the file's measured 373266. It stood at 640000, the 2026-07-18 seed above the then-current size with rotation headroom; the July chapters rotated to docs/queue-archive/JOURNAL-archive-2026-07-29.md and the headroom went unspent, so it is withdrawn. Old dated chapters keep rotating to a dated archive; a session that must write more than the ceiling allows rotates first or raises this number with a reason. One byte above rather than exactly at the size, because tests/test_doc_bound.py asserts the strict inequality."
        },
        "ARCHITECTURE.md": {
            "max_bytes": 108130,
            "reason": "raised 2026-08-18 from 107774 to 108130, the file's measured size exactly, for 356 bytes of architecture. Eight pins and one invariant line had counted the wired judges wrong from 2026-08-17 on. The wired-hook declaration's pin now says two hooks are wired by default and six are opt-in; the shared turn reader's pin says none of its five readers is wired by default; the hedge arm's line says the setup walk places its file while the host wires it; and the six hooks' own pins carry the opt-in/library marker their sibling lean-orchestrator pin already carried, so a reader comparing pins no longer reads the six as default-wired. No node could hold the growth instead, because each fact belongs to the pin whose file it describes. Before that: raised 2026-08-18 from 107652 to 107774, the file's measured size exactly, for 122 bytes of which not one is content. The ratchet's down-only rule is a law about what a document SAYS, not about how long the paths inside it are, and both of that night's additions are path length. The first, 48 bytes: six guardrail checks were parked in guardrails/attic/, and the eight places here that aim at them \u2014 six pins and two prose mentions \u2014 each gained the six characters of `attic/`. The second, 74 bytes: six pins into the spec-author skill were re-aimed at paths inside references/ after that skill's sections moved there. Both are the price of tidying up, and this field exists for exactly that. No word of architecture was added in either, and no node could have held the growth instead, because the growth IS the pins staying true to where their files now sit. The budget row's live test count was also refreshed twice that night, 2,506 to 2,532 to the 2,555 the merged tree collects; every one of those figures is the same width, so the refreshes cost nothing. No margin beyond the measured size was taken. Before that: ratcheted down 2026-08-17 to 107652, the file's measured size exactly. This entry never carried headroom: seeded 2026-08-15 for the readability milestone of the accepted plan v2 (2026-08-14) at that day's measured 107845, on the stated convention that the ceiling is the measured size and the ratchet points down only. The file has since shrunk by 193 bytes, so the ceiling follows it down. Exactly at the size rather than one byte above, because ARCHITECTURE.md is not among tests/test_doc_bound.py's FOUR_DOCS and the gate itself tolerates equality. The four large documents got a hundred kilobytes of room because they were already past reading size and rotation was the remedy; the architecture is still readable at ~105 KB, and the milestone's whole point is that it stays that way. A node that must grow pays for the growth here, by name: raise this number with a reason that says what was added and why no node could hold it instead."
        }
    }
}

